docs/architecture/napi-binding.md:17:- `session_warm_up(session_id, count) -> u32` — resolves `count` components spread evenly over the session's keys, without returning them, so a lazy session holds the dependency declarations and resolver memos components share before the first request; the addon uses it for `warmUp` and `fillAfter`. Returns how many it resolved; an eager session, an unknown session and a count of 0 resolve nothing. Runs inside `spawn_blocking`.
docs/type-checker-integration.md:106:A long-lived session (the Storybook addon, the lazy `WatchSession`) attaches a warm checker instead of running the whole-project pass above (ADR 0022): a napi session does unless `checker: false`. `caliper-docgen-semantic` keeps one checker warm behind a worker thread, answers from a program rooted at only the requested component's file, and returns its props. The program's snapshot is kept between requests and brought up to date with the files the session says changed ("Watch", below). `WatchSession::component` merges them into the structural answer by name (the checker's type wins, a structural prop the checker did not return stays, and `htmlAttributes` applies once after the merge, as in the structural pass) when they arrive within `semanticWaitMs` (default 150 ms), or within `semanticWaitEmptyMs` (default 2,000 ms, never less than `semanticWaitMs`) when the structural answer lists no props at all (a `ComponentProps<typeof Button>` of a package the structural pass does not model, whose props type it could not expand); otherwise it returns the structural answer plus an `Info` diagnostic, and the checker's late answer serves the next request of the same component while no source file has changed. A kept answer carries what the checker said about the request that made it (a warning that it was not told about an edit, why a component kept its structural result), and every request it serves reports that again, a late answer's included; only an unavailable checker's warning is said once per session. There is one request in flight per component key and content hash of its file: a caller that asks while it is in flight (a story asked while its own prefetch runs, or two stories of one component) waits for that answer, so the checker answers it once. The checker answers interactive requests (`WatchSession::component`, `components`) before background ones (`components_in_background`, which `sessionWarmUp`, the addon's `warmUp` and `fillAfter` use, and which waits for nobody), first come first served within each; an interactive caller that joins a background request raises it, and after 4 interactive answers in a row while background requests wait, the oldest of them goes next. Any source-file change, including a file the component only imports, drops every checker answer, so the next request asks again; an answer still in flight at that moment is neither kept nor joined. The component's structural diagnostics that the checker's props supersede are dropped when they replace the structural ones, except while a structural prop the checker did not return is still opaque: those diagnostics name the component, not the prop, so they stay until nothing they could be about is left. (The whole-project merge drops unreturned structural props, so it has no such case.) Which ones are superseded is in "Diagnostics a checker answer supersedes".
docs/measurement.md:239:The settings are the Storybook addon's (`createDocgenProvider` in `packages/storybook/src/provider.ts`): dependency types loaded, `collapseInheritedProps: 'none'`, a lazy session, and the NAPI defaults for the rest (`htmlAttributes: 'full'`, the on-disk cache on). The CLI runs passed the same props settings (`--load-dependencies --collapse-inherited-props none --html-attributes full`) so that the structural and the checker run list the same props. The sessions left out the per-story look at the files (`ensureFresh`, a few milliseconds on a large project) and `warmUp`/`fillAfter`, both off in the addon by default.
packages/storybook/README.md:43:| `warmUp` | `false` | With `lazy`, resolve a few components in the background after start. Off: it did not make the first story faster in measurement. |
packages/storybook/README.md:45:| `checker` | `true` | Keep the built-in TypeScript checker warm and ask it for the component a story shows, so its props are the type checker's (shadcn's components gain more than twice as many props). A story waits up to `semanticWaitMs` for it and otherwise shows caliper-docgen's own structural props; the checker's answer is kept for the next request. The checker answers the stories you open before `warmUp` and `fillAfter` work, and a story whose component it is already answering waits for that answer instead of asking again. Nothing to install: the checker is part of the addon's native binding. `false` never starts it. (It replaces `semanticTypes`, which the addon now refuses.) |

